  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 256 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nFriday - Saturday...\n\nThe surface cold front will be stalled near the KY/TN border by \nFriday morning. Meanwhile, a positively tilted shortwave trough will \napproach and slowly move through the region. Expect lingering \nshowers and a few storm chances on Friday ahead of this trough axis, \nmainly confined to central and southern KY. Overall, deep layer \nshear will be pretty weak by this point, so not expecting any \norganized updrafts. Temperatures behind the front will be notably \ncooler with highs on Friday in the low and mid 80s.\n\nLooks like mainly diurnally driven showers or a storm chance \ncontinues into Saturday as another slow moving shortwave passes \nthrough the zonal flow aloft. Temps look to recover back to the 85 \nto 90 degree range as we enter the heart of the weekend.\n\nSunday - Tuesday...\n\nThe center of the upper ridge looks to retrograde slightly back into \nthe Carolinas late weekend into early next week, helping to push the \nstronger belt of westerlies farther north. The increased upper \nridging will come with temperatures likely holding above normal in \nthe upper 80s and low 90s. Looks to be a somewhat \"dirty\" ridge with \nnot much of a subsidence inversion to keep vertical development in \ncheck. Won't be a lot of triggering mechanisms either, outside of \ndiurnal heating, so will only expect some isolated afternoon and \nevening shower or storm activity each day. Vertical shear will be \nweak so any storm modes would be pulse in nature.",
